had an esophagogastroduodenoscopy in the past year for peptic ulcer disease. She is doing well with this.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports left elbow pain, stiffness, and swelling.\n• Neurological: Denies weakness, numbness, or tingling.\n• Respiratory: Endorses nasal congestion.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck: Supple without midline tenderness.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n• Musculoskeletal: Left elbow shows erythema and edema. Pain to palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable right radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLeft elbow X-ray: No evidence of any elbow fracture.\n\nEsophagogastroduodenoscopy: Revealed gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMs. Anna Smith is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease, who presents with left elbow pain.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well with her Keppra 500 mg twice daily.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice daily. Obtain a refill.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start her on Lexapro 10 mg once daily. She will follow up in 2 weeks.\n\nPeptic Ulcer Disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: She had an esophagogastroduodenoscopy in the past year for peptic ulcer disease. Her last one resolved after taking Protonix.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily prior to meals.\n\nLeft Elbow Pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has repetitive trauma to her left elbow from typing at work.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue meloxicam 15 mg once daily. Ice the area.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up