copy in the past year for peptic ulcer disease. She is doing well with this.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fevers or chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports left elbow pain, stiffness, and swelling.\n• Neurological: Denies weakness, numbness, or tingling.\n• Respiratory: Endorses nasal congestion.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck: Supple without midline tenderness.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\n• Musculoskeletal: Left elbow shows erythema and edema. Pain to palpation of the olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. Palpable right radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLeft elbow X-ray: No evidence of any elbow fracture.\n\nEsophagogastroduodenoscopy: Revealed gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMs. Anna Smith is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease, who presents with left elbow pain.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well with her Keppra 500 mg twice daily.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice daily. Obtain a refill.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She is doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: We will start her on Lexapro 10 mg once daily. She will follow up in 2 weeks.\n\nPeptic Ulcer Disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: She had an esophagogastroduodenoscopy in the past year for peptic ulcer disease. Her last one resolved after taking Protonix.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily prior to meals.\n\nLeft Elbow Pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has repetitive trauma to her left elbow from typing at work.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue meloxicam 15 mg once daily. Ice the area.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nAnna is a 26-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of left elbow pain. She is a right-handed individual who works at a desk and types frequently throughout the day. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands or weakness in her arm. She denies any fever or chills. She has had no seizures recently and continues to take Keppra 500 mg twice a day. She has been in therapy for her depression and has been doing well.\n\nThe patient endorses some nasal congestion due to allergies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever or chills.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports left elbow pain.\n• Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling in her hands or weakness in her arm.\n• Respiratory: Reports nasal congestion due to allergies.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow reveals erythema and edema of the left elbow. Pain to palpation of the olecranon. The left elbow is warm to palpation. The patient has pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow. There is a palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Left arm x-ray: No evidence of any elbow fracture.\n\n• Endoscopy: The endoscopy results show gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Left Elbow Pain: The patient presents with left elbow pain, which is consistent with olecranon bursitis. She will use a pillow for her elbow and take meloxicam 15 mg once a day. She can ice the area.\n2. Epilepsy: The patient is doing well and will continue taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n3. Depression: The patient is doing well with therapy and will start taking lexapro 10 mg once a day.\n4. Peptic Ulcer Disease: The patient will continue taking protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements\n\n• She understands the recommended treatment plan for her left elbow pain.\n• She understands the recommended treatment plan for her epilepsy.\n• She understands the recommended treatment plan for her depression.\n• She understands the recommended treatment plan for her peptic ulcer disease.\n\nPatient Education\n\n• She will use a pillow for her elbow.\n• She will take meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\n• She can ice the area.\n• She will continue taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n• She will start taking lexapro 10 mg once a day.\n• She will continue taking protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\n• Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n• Lexapro 10 mg once a day.\n• Protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\n• Continue to use a pillow for her elbow.\n• Continue taking meloxicam 15 mg once a day.\n• Continue to ice the area.\n• Continue taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n• Start taking lexapro 10 mg once a day.\n• Continue taking protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n• Patient is a 26-year-old female who presents with left elbow pain.\n• Patient is a right-handed individual who works at a desk and types frequently throughout the day.\n• Patient denies any numbness or tingling in her hands or weakness in her arm.\n• Patient denies any fever or chills.\n• Patient has had no seizures recently and continues to take Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n• Patient has been in therapy for her depression and has been doing well.\n• Patient endorses some nasal congestion due to allergies.\n• Patient has erythema and edema of the left elbow.\n• Patient has pain to palpation of the olecranon.\n• Patient has pain with flexion and extension of the left elbow.\n• Patient has a palpable left radial pulse.\n• Patient has gastritis.\n• Patient is doing well and will continue taking Keppra 500 mg twice a day.\n• Patient is doing well with therapy and will start taking lexapro 10 mg once a day.\n• Patient will continue taking protonix 40 mg once a day before meals.\n• Patient has no evidence of any elbow fracture on her left arm x-ray.\n• Patient has gastritis on her endoscopy. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nThe patient is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with some joint pain.\n\nThe patient reports that her left elbow has really been bothering her. She states that she sits at her desk all day for work with her elbows bent. She reports that after work it is really hard for her to flex and extend her left elbow. She reports that it has been really stiff and painful. She also notes that she has noticed swelling on the olecranon of her elbow. She reports that she is right-handed, therefore, she can still write, which is great, but typing is difficult. She denies any numbness or tingling in her hands. She reports that she has some weakness in her arm but nothing too significant. She denies any fever or chills.\n\nRegarding her epilepsy, she reports that she has not had any recent seizures. She reports that her last seizure was a year ago. She reports that she is still taking Keppra.\n\nRegarding her depression, she reports that she started therapy last year and she is trying to avoid medications for this problem. She reports that therapy has been helpful, but she still feels down and stressed. She denies suicidal and homicidal ideations.\n\nRegarding her peptic ulcer, she reports that she was dealing with a lot of stress last year. She then underwent an endoscopy which showed that she had an episode of gastritis. She was subsequently diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease and started Protonix. She reports that she has not had any issues with her ulcer since the procedure and everything has been good.\n\nThe patient denies chest pain, shortness of breath, abdominal pain, nausea, or vomiting. She reports that she does have a little bit of medial nasal congestion from allergies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Ears, Nose, Mouth and Throat: Endorses medial nasal congestion from allergies.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or dyspnea on exertion.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Musculoskeletal: Endorses left elbow joint pain, swelling, and weakness.\n• Psychiatric: Endorses depression.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the left elbow reveals erythema and edema. Pain to palpation of the left olecranon. Warm to palpation. Pain with flexion and extension. Palpable left radial pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLeft arm x-ray does not reveal any evidence of elbow fracture.\n\nEGD revealed an episode of gastritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAnna Morris is a 26-year-old female with a past medical history significant for epilepsy, depression, and peptic ulcer disease who presents with some joint pain.\n\nPLAN\n\nLeft elbow pain.\n• Medical Reasoning: I believe she has left olecranon bursitis caused by repetitive trauma.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate meloxicam 15 mg once daily.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to use a pillow to rest her arm on. She may also ice the area.\n\nEpilepsy.\n• Medical Reasoning: She has been doing well on Keppra and denies any recent seizures.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Keppra 500 mg twice a day. This was refilled today.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: She reports doing well with therapy.\n• Medical Treatment: Initiate Lexapro 10 mg once daily.\n\nPeptic ulcer disease.\n• Medical Reasoning: Her recent endoscopy revealed gastritis\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Protonix 40 mg once daily before meals.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
