anti-inflammatories , um , go ahead and take some ibuprofen , 600 milligrams four times a day with food . i do want you to do some straight leg raising exercises , that\'ll strengthen your quadriceps and that\'ll help with that pain , okay ?\n[patient] okay .\n[doctor] um , basically you want to avoid anything that puts extra pressure on your knees for a little while and it should be fine . if it\'s not getting any better in a couple weeks i wan na see you again , okay ? so no lunges , avoid the breaststroke . if you\'re comfortable, you can also do other swimming strokes where your knees are straight instead . or you can take the opportunity to have a little break .\n[patient] all right .\n[doctor] your second problem is your chronic back pain , that\'s been doing really well . um , keep swimming , that\'s okay after this gets better .\n[patient] yeah .\n[doctor] um , but i think , um , i think that\'s been under control . but if it flares up go ahead and , uh , give me a call , okay ?\n[patient] mm-hmm .\n[doctor] um , your third problem is your depression . that\'s been stable . we\'re gon na keep you on your current medication , not gon na change anything at this point , okay ? and as far as your carpal tunnel goes , that\'s relatively stable , so , um , uh , no changes there as well . okay ? so anything else i can do for you ?\n[patient] no , that\'s it .\n[doctor] okay , well we\'ll get you checked out .\n[patient] all right .\n[doctor] hey dragon , finalize the report .\n\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMs. Rebecca Brown is a 27-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right knee pain. The onset of her pain started 1 month ago after she began swimming more regularly. She denies any injury or trauma to her knee. She notes that she is only having pain in her right knee while swimming. She is using the breaststroke technique with her legs kicking out. She is able to swim 10 laps initially, but after a couple of laps, she starts to feel pain in her right knee. She denies any pain down her leg.\n\nMs. Brown has a past medical history of carpal tunnel syndrome status post carpal tunnel release, chronic back pain, and depression. She has been taking medication for her depression and has been seeing a therapist. She has been doing well with this. She has been getting outside more and has been doing well with her back pain. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations.\n\nMs. Brown denies any knee pain outside of swimming. She denies any back pain. She denies any pain down her legs. She denies any joint swelling.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nShe is currently taking medication for depression.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight changes.\nEENT: Denies dysphagia.\nPsychiatric: Denies suicidal or homicidal ideations.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies joint swelling.\nSkin: Denies rashes.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: No swelling. Pain with patellar inhibition. Pain with range of motion of the knee.\nMSK: Examination of the back: No midline tenderness. No pain with straight leg raising.\nNeuro: Normal strength in the lower extremities. No numbness or tingling.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right knee is reviewed and is normal.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nChondromalacia of the patella.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is experiencing right knee pain after swimming. She has been swimming more regularly for the past month. She has a history of carpal tunnel syndrome status post carpal tunnel release. I believe her symptoms are due to chondromalacia of the patella.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on medication and seeing a therapist. She is doing well with this. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations. I believe her depression is well-controlled at this time.\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome status post carpal tunnel release.\n• Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing well with this.\n\nPLAN\n\nChondromalacia of the patella.\n• Medical Treatment: I am prescribing ibuprofen 600 mg four times a day with food. I would like her to avoid activities that put extra pressure on her knees. I would like her to do straight leg raising exercises to strengthen her quadriceps. I would like her to avoid the breaststroke while swimming. If she is comfortable, she can try other swimming strokes where her knees are straight. I would like her to avoid lunges. I would like her to follow up in 2 weeks if her symptoms do not improve.\n\nDepression.\n• Medical Treatment: I am going to keep her on her current medication regimen.\n\nCarpal tunnel syndrome status post carpal tunnel release.\n• Medical Treatment: No changes at this time.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nPlease follow up in 2 weeks if her symptoms do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nKnee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nRebecca is a 27-year-old female who presents for follow-up for a right knee pain. She has a history of carpal tunnel syndrome that has been doing well. She also has a history of depression that has been stable.\n\nShe has been swimming more regularly over the past month and has started to feel right knee pain. The pain is localized to her right knee and is exacerbated by swimming. She has been able to swim 10 laps without pain, but after a couple of laps, she starts to feel pain. She denies any injury to her knee. She denies any pain going down her legs. She denies any numbness or tingling in her feet.\n\nShe has a history of depression that has been stable. She denies any suicidal ideation. She is currently on medication for depression and has been doing well. She has been sleeping well and eating well. She has been exercising outside more and has been feeling really well.\n\nShe has had a history of carpal tunnel release a couple of years ago. She has been doing well with that. She has had full range of motion and has been doing well.\