, we will start her on insulin.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in a month. She will continue her metoprolol 25 mg twice a day for her atrial fibrillation. She will continue her protonix 40 mg once a day for her gastroesophageal reflux. She will start metformin 500 mg twice a day for her diabetes. She will also need a refill on her metoprolol. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nHigh blood sugar.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nHannah is a 52-year-old female who presents for evaluation of high blood sugar. She reports that she has been eating a lot of sweets lately and has noticed symptoms of lightheadedness and seeing spots. She went to the emergency room where they treated her with fluids and advised her to cut back on sweets and resume her normal diet. She is having trouble with this because her family members are big bakers. She reports that she has osteoarthritis and atrial fibrillation.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Reports of lightheadedness and seeing spots.\n• Cardiovascular: Reports of atrial fibrillation. Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain, but reports reflux.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports of osteoarthritis.\n• Neurological: Denies headache or muscle aches.\n• Respiratory: Denies cough or shortness of breath.\n• Urinary: Denies burning with urination.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: She is in no distress. Her thyroid is slightly enlarged, but nontender. There are no carotid bruits. Her heart is in an irregular rate and rhythm. Her lungs are clear. Her abdomen is soft. She has no edema in her lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Blood Glucose: 250 mg/dL.\n• Hemoglobin A1C: 8.3%.\n• Diabetes labs: Results pending.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nDiabetes Mellitus: The patient presents with a high blood sugar. She is currently on metoprolol, 25 mg, twice a day, 