ed flexion and extension. Strength in lower extremities is good.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the left knee is normal.\n\nASPECS\n\nPlan\n\nWe discussed the patient\'s left knee pain today. I have ordered an autoimmune profile and physical therapy. We will start her on meloxicam 15 mg once a day. We will continue her Norvasc, Metformin, and Lipitor.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nJoint pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJanet is a 33-year-old female who presents for evaluation of joint pain. She has been doing stair workouts to build muscle on her knees and has been experiencing bilateral knee pain. She denies any other joint pain such as in her elbows or shoulders. She does have a history of right rotator cuff repair two years ago, but has not had any issues since that time. She does have diabetes and monitors her blood sugars regularly. She has not had any fever, chills, nausea or vomiting, chest pain or shortness of breath.\n\nShe does take Norvasc 5mg daily and has been taking it every day. She does have high blood pressure, but her blood sugars have been under control. She has been taking medication for it and her blood pressure has not elevated.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, nausea, vomiting, chest pain, or shortness of breath.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain. Reports high blood pressure.\n• Endocrine: Reports diabetes.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\n• Neck: Supple, no nuchal rigidity.\n• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\n• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n• Musculoskeletal: Left knee: Erythema and edema are present. Small effusion is palpable. Decreased flexion and extension of the knee. Strength in the lower extremities is intact.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• Blood