ssment and plan is for for this\n[patient] no no questions today\n[doctor] okay now the other thing\n[patient] how soon can i how soon will i be able to get up and get moving back outside after the surgery\n[doctor] so the good news is since it\'s a laparoscopic procedure you\'re gon na be back on your feet pretty quick now for the first twenty four hours i\'m gon na want you to take it pretty easy but after a couple of days if you feel like moving around on some solid ground you know we do n\'t want you hiking or anything like that but if you feel like moving around on some solid ground you can get up and walk around within a day or two and then in about a week or so i\'ll see you again and then we\'ll release you to go get back out there in nature\n[patient] okay thank you very much\n[doctor] you\'re very welcome the other thing i wan na talk briefly about is now your not your type two diabetes i want you to follow up with your physician just to make sure that you\'re handling your diabetes especially the fact that you\'re gon na have surgery and those type of things and we will just continue to monitor your diabetes as has been outlined by your your additional either your endocrinologist or your primary care provider i\'m gon na have the nurse come in and get some paperwork signed and if you do n\'t have any questions for me i will see you next week and we will get this gallbladder out\n[patient] well thank you very much i\'m looking forward to i i do n\'t want anymore of that pain so\n[doctor] alright that sounds good we\'ll see you next week then\n[patient] okay take care\n[doctor] yeah\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby Miller is a pleasant 67-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for follow-up of his abdominal pain. The patient was seen by Dr. Miller in the office 3 weeks ago for the same complaint. The patient reports that he has been having pain on the right side of his abdomen, specifically underneath his ribs. He describes it as a squeezing pain that comes and goes. He rates his pain as an 8 out of 10 in severity. He states that it is associated with nausea. He denies any fevers or vomiting. The patient reports that he has a history of type 2 diabetes and was initially going to be managed with diet and exercise, but he has since started a keto diet which has resulted in a 25-pound weight loss over the past 3 months. The patient has a family history of gallbladder disease, both his mother and father required cholecystectomy.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports abdominal pain. Denies nausea and vomiting.\nGenitourinary: Denies hematuria.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies back pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, clicks, or murmurs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended. Positive bowel sounds. Slight guarding in the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound shows multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Mild wall thickening is appreciated. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiologic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I explained to him that he has mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I explained that he has not had any improvement in his symptoms despite dietary modifications. I have recommended a laparoscopic cholecystectomy to remove his gallbladder. He will undergo the procedure in the next week. He will spend the night in the hospital for postoperative monitoring. He will need to avoid high fat foods after the surgery. I also discussed his type 2 diabetes with him today. I advised him to follow up with his primary care physician to ensure he is managing his diabetes well.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 1 week after his surgery. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nPain in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nBobby is a 52-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for a follow-up of his right upper quadrant abdominal pain. The patient states that he has been having this pain for several weeks. The patient reports that his primary care physician, Dr. Miller, sent him to see me today. The patient states that Dr. Miller did an ultrasound of his gallbladder and found gallstones. Dr. Miller recommended that he avoid high-fat foods, but the patient is still having pain. Dr. Miller referred him to me today.\n\nThe patient reports that his pain is on the right-hand side of his abdomen, underneath his ribs. The patient describes this pain as a squeezing, severe pain. He states that it comes and goes, and when it comes, it is severe and he gets nauseated and sick in his stomach. The patient rates his pain as an 8 out of 10 when it is severe.\n\nThe patient states that the pain is related to eating. He reports that he eats right after meals and the pain occurs. He also states that it can occur a couple of hours after he eats. The patient states that he has tried to avoid fatty foods and high-fat foods, but the pain still persists.\n\nThe patient reports that he is a type 2 diabetic. He reports that he was diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and that he has been doing well. He reports that he has been doing a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds in the last three months. The patient states that his diet was worse when he was first diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. He reports that since then, he has cut back on sugar and started a keto diet and has lost 25 pounds. The patient states that he loves to eat and that he enjoys photography. He reports that he loves to go out and take nature pictures.\n\nThe patient reports that he has had a family history of gallbladder disease. He reports that both his father and mother had their gallbladders removed.\n\nThe patient reports that he has been experiencing some fevers. He reports that he has had some low-grade fevers and that he has been nauseated, but he has not thrown up.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nConstitutional: The patient appears to be in no acute distress.\n\nRespiratory: Auscultation of the lungs reveals clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular: Auscultation of the heart reveals a regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats, clicks, or murmurs are noted.\n\nGastrointestinal: The patient has a soft, nontender abdomen. The patient has positive bowel sounds. The patient has slight guarding to the right upper quadrant. When I press beneath the patient\'s right rib cage, the patient reports pain.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound reveals multiple gallstones in the gallbladder. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nI believe that the patient has mild cholecystitis with gallstones. The patient has not had any improvement with dietary modifications. I am going to recommend that we remove the patient\'s gallbladder. I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. The surgery will take about an hour to an hour and a half. The patient will probably spend the night in the hospital to monitor the patient post-procedure. The patient will need to avoid high-fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber.\n\nThe patient has no questions about the assessment and plan.\n\nI will see the patient next week.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nNone.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure: 128/88 mmHg\nRespiratory rate: 16\nPulse: 68\nOxygen saturation on room air: 99%\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound: Multiple gallstones in the gallbladder. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nMild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n\nPLAN\n\nI recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. The surgery will take about an hour to an hour and a half. The patient will probably spend the night in the hospital to monitor the patient post-procedure. The patient will need to avoid high-fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber.\n\nI will see the patient next week. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, follo