 right side i do n\'t hear any wheezing right now there is some diminished breath sounds in the right side as well on your heart exam you\'ve got two over successolic ejection murmur you\'ve had that in the past that\'s unchanged from before the rest regular rate and rhythm otherwise your belly exam is nice and soft on your extremity exam you\'ve got one plus nonpitting edema on both of your lower extremities on your on your ankles no calf tenderness no negative homan sign so what does all this mean so basically you\'ve got you know you\'ve got some sort of infection or something going on in your lungs that i can hear right now the rest of your exam is pretty much stable it\'s unchanged from before so let\'s talk about what we what we should do about this so for the for the first problem with the shortness of breath the first thing i wan na do is go ahead and get a chest x-ray for you okay you\'ve been coughing up blood i also wan na send you to the emergency department to get some blood testing done i\'m worried about a blood clot or something else going on so i wan na get a cat scan of your chest as well so i\'m gon na go ahead and refer you to the emergency department i\'ll call them and and have you head over that way they can get a chest x-ray and a cat scan and some blood work as well and then we\'ll evaluate why you\'re having the shortness of breath and why you\'re coughing up this blood okay and then depending on what they find you may need you know it could be as simple as a pneumonia or it could be something a little bit more serious we may have to get you know start you know depending on what you find we will get the right treatment started any questions about that or can you drive to the emergency department from here or is that okay\n[patient] yeah i can bike no problem i have no question\n[doctor] alright for the second problem for the diabetes since your blood sugar is running a little bit high once you get over this i\'m blood sugars running a little bit high once we get you over this hump of the shortness of breath and get this diagnosed i\'m gon na go ahead and order another hemoglobin a1c today we will probably increase your dose of metformin from five hundred twice a day to seven fifty twice a day so why do n\'t we do that and then why do n\'t we have we\'ll have the hemoglobin a1c back and then we can once you get over this illness that you\'re undergoing we\'ll we\'ll we\'ll discuss that more i\'ll have you come back in about three weeks to just discuss your diabetes and what our treatment plan will be for that okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] for the high blood pressure definitely hold off on the norvasc for right now let\'s figure out what\'s going what\'s causing this episode of shortness of breath i want some blood testing done and and once we have that under better control we can reassess your blood pressure so right now hold the norvasc and then we\'ll have you come back once everything is done okay\n[patient] no\n[doctor] any other questions or anything we know if we need to talk about today\n[patient] no i\'m good\n[doctor] sounds good we\'ll get you signed out here in a second\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMr. Dennis Smith is a 57-year-old male who presents today with a chief complaint of shortness of breath. He has a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma.\n\nMr. Smith reports that he has been experiencing shortness of breath for the past couple of weeks. He wakes up with shortness of breath and also experiences it when he tries to exert himself. He has also been coughing up blood, about 3 to 4 cups per day. He has not noticed any bruising or bleeding from his gums or teeth. He has been to the office, but was unable to get an appointment. He denies any fevers.\n\nMr. Smith has been experiencing intermittent knee pain for quite some time now. He denies any swelling. He denies any recent travel history.\n\nMr. Smith is on metformin 500 mg twice daily for his diabetes. He is also on Norvasc for his hypertension. He has a dexcom device that monitors his blood sugar levels. He reports that his blood sugar levels spike after meals, up to 200. He denies any low blood sugars. He states that his blood pressure has been running somewhat low, but he does not always take his medications.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports fatigue.\nCardiovascular: Reports dyspnea on exertion.\nRespiratory: Reports dyspnea on exertion and hemoptysis.\nGastrointestinal: Denies hematochezia.\nGenitourinary: Denies hematuria.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No JVD. No carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Bilateral rales and rhonchi more on the right side. No wheezing. Diminished breath sounds on the right side.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: 2/6 ejection murmur. Unchanged from previous exam. Regular rate and rhythm.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft and nontender.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema in both lower extremities. No calf tenderness. No Homan sign.\n\nRESULTS\n\nOxygen level in the clinic today was 94% on pulse oximetry.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMr. Dennis Smith is a 57-year-old male who presents today with a chief complaint of shortness of breath. He has a history of diabetes, hypertension, and asthma.\n\nShortness of breath.\n- Medical Reasoning: He has been experiencing shortness of breath for the past couple of weeks. He also reports coughing up blood. His oxygen level in the office today was 94% on pulse oximetry. On physical exam, he has rhonchi and diminished breath sounds on the right side.\n- Medical Treatment: We will refer him to the emergency department for a chest X-ray and a CT scan. He will also get some blood work done. Once he gets over this episode of shortness of breath, we will discuss his diabetes and treatment plan.\n\nDiabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His blood sugar levels have been running high, up to 200 after meals. He has a dexcom device that monitors his blood sugar levels. We will increase his dose of metformin from 500 mg twice daily to 750 mg twice daily.\n- Medical Treatment: We will increase his dose of metformin from 500 mg twice daily to 750 mg twice daily. We will repeat his hemoglobin A1C today.\n\nHypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: He has been experiencing intermittent low blood pressures. He is currently on Norvasc.\n- Medical Treatment: Hold the Norvasc for now. We will reassess his blood pressure once he gets over this episode of shortness of breath.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nShortness of breath.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis is a 57-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of shortness of breath. He reports that he has been feeling short of breath for the past couple of weeks. He states that he wakes up with shortness of breath and it persists even when he is at home. He reports that he has been coughing up blood for the past three weeks. He denies any chest pain, fever, chills, or recent travel. He has a history of diabetes and high blood pressure. He denies any leg swelling, bruising, or bleeding from his teeth or gums.\n\nThe patient states that he tried to get an appointment in the office but they were booked up. He denies any history of shortness of breath or difficulty breathing when he walks or exerts himself. He reports that his blood sugars have been running high and that he has been wearing a dexcom. He states that his blood sugars have been spiking up after meals and that his morning blood sugars are around 150. He denies any recent changes in his diet or exercise routine.\n\nThe patient states that his blood pressure has been running somewhat low and that he is taking norvasc.