n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Thompson is a pleasant 38-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for an evaluation of right knee injury. The onset of his pain began 3 days ago when he was playing soccer and felt a pop in his right knee. He has swelling, pain, and limited range of motion. The patient states that he can bear weight on his right leg but it feels loose and does not feel normal. He is currently on ibuprofen for pain relief. The patient reports his pain is a 6-7 out of 10, and he has been taking ibuprofen and it has helped. He has full range of motion in his left knee. He denies any type of locking or buckling of his right knee.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain and swelling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK\n- Examination of the right knee: There is a marked limp upon ambulation. I do appreciate a significant effusion in the right knee. There is a slight bit of ecchymosis. Pain to palpation over the medial joint line. He has some difficulty with passive range of motion. Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to about 115 degrees as well as lacking about 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to the pain. Positive Lachman exam.\n\nNEURO\n- Examination of the right lower extremity: Normal strength 5/5.\n- Examination of the left lower extremity: Normal strength 5/5.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right knee were reviewed and were normal.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAnterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended an MRI to evaluate the integrity of his ACL. I have prescribed meloxicam 50 mg daily to help reduce his pain and swelling. I have also advised him to ice his knee as needed. I have instructed him to limit his activity, wear a knee immobilizer, and use crutches when ambulating. I have also instructed him to limit his jogging until we get the results of his MRI. The patient has also mentioned that he has a dog that he would like to continue to play with. I have advised him to avoid that as well.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once he has his MRI results. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee pain and swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott is a 30-year-old male who presents to the office for right knee pain and swelling. He reports that he was playing soccer over the weekend and went to kick the ball and felt a pop in his right knee. He states that he was trying to play soccer and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports that the pain is about a 6 out of 10. He states that he took ibuprofen and that it helped a little bit. He states that he is still having pain and swelling in the right knee. He states that he can bend it but it hurts towards the end of the motion and he can straighten it but it does not feel normal. He states that he can bear weight on it but it feels loose.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain and swelling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMusculoskeletal: Right knee: The patient has a marked limp on the right leg. There is significant effusion in the right knee. There is slight ecchymosis. The patient reports pain on palpation to the medial joint line. He has limited range of motion of the right knee with 115 degrees of terminal extension and 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion. The patient has a positive Lachman exam.\n\nRESULTS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Right knee: There is significant effusion in the right knee. There is slight ecchymosis. The patient reports pain on palpation to the medial joint line. He has limited range of motion of the right knee with 115 degrees of terminal extension and 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion. The patient has a positive Lachman exam.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nRight knee pain and swelling.\n\nRight knee sprain.\n\nRight knee effu