 few months. She states that she is compliant with her medications and has an alarm to remind her to take them. She also has a history of diabetes mellitus, which is controlled with metformin 500 mg twice daily. She admits to having a sweet tooth and has been consuming higher amounts of sugar lately. She also enjoys baking and making cakes and scones. She has been doing this since she was young. The patient states that she has had a diabetic consult in the past and is interested in speaking with a dietitian again.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers.\nCardiovascular: Denies orthopnea.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\nGastrointestinal: Denies abdominal pain. Endorses dyspepsia.\nPsychiatric: Denies anxiety.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\nLungs: Clear bilaterally to auscultation.\n\nCardiovascular\nNo murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Equal pulses bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal\nNo bruising, swelling, or discoloration of the right elbow. Tenderness to palpation over the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and extension of the right wrist.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right elbow is reviewed and reveals no bony abnormalities.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain.\n2. Diabetes mellitus.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nRight elbow pain.\n- Medical Reasoning:\n- Patient reports right elbow pain while making a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. X-ray of the right elbow reveals no bony abnormalities. This is consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n- Medical Treatment:\n- Obtain a sling to wear while awake. Apply ice to the right elbow for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. Take 600 mg of ibuprofen, 3 times a day for 1 week. Hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the nature of the injury and the treatment plan in detail. I advised the patient to rest the right elbow and avoid pottery for the next couple of weeks.\n\nDiabetes mellitus.\n- Medical Reasoning:\n- Patient reports that she is having difficulty with blood glucose control. She has a sweet tooth and enjoys baking. She is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment:\n- Increase metformin to 1000 mg, twice daily. Obtain an A1C. Follow-up in 2 weeks.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of monitoring her blood glucose levels and the importance of making lifestyle changes to better control her diabetes.\n\nHypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning:\n- Patient is compliant with her medications and has a good blood pressure control.\n- Medical Treatment:\n- Continue lisinopril 10 mg, daily.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining her current blood pressure control and the importance of lifestyle modifications to prevent the progression of her hypertension.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks for her diabetes mellitus. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nMEDICAL REASON\n\nThe patient is a 64-year-old female who presents to the clinic for evaluation of right elbow pain. She reports that the pain started 4 days ago and has been worsening. She describes the pain as a 7 out of 10, which is constant and is worse with activity. She denies any trauma or injury to the elbow. She reports that she has been doing pottery and ceramics and has been having trouble with her arm and elbow pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient denies any smoking, and she does not use any illicit substances. She drinks 1-2 glasses of wine per week. She denies any history of drug use.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\n• Lisinopril 10 mg daily\n• Metformin 500 mg twice daily\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, and weight loss.\n• Musculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n• Neurological: Denies headache, dizziness, and paresthesias.\n• Respiratory: Denies cough, shortness of breath, and wheezing.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION\n\n• Respiratory: Auscultation of 