ally.\n• Cardiovascular: Auscultation of the heart is normal.\n• Musculoskeletal: Right elbow pain to palpation of the medial epicondyle. Pain to resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain to flexion and extension of the right wrist.\n• Neurological: Normal motor and sensory exam of the upper extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\n• X-ray of the right elbow: No fracture or bony abnormality.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Right elbow pain. Medical epicondylitis. The patient is to apply ice to the right elbow for 20 minutes, 3 times a day. She is to take 600 mg of ibuprofen, 3 times a day, for 1 week. She is to wear a sling while awake. She is to avoid pottery and ceramics for 2 weeks.\n• Diabetes. The patient is to increase her metformin to 1000 mg, 2 times a day. She is to check her blood sugars daily and to follow up with the provider in 2 weeks.\n• Hypertension. The patient is to continue on lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with the provider in 2 weeks for her diabetes and elbow pain. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 1