d the pain continues i\'m gon na go ahead and maybe order a cat scan and then at that point in time we can refer you to a pain doctor if we\'re still having significant pain\n[patient] sounds good\n[doctor] okay thank you i\'m gon na send my nursing to see you and we\'ll get you out the door\n[patient] thank you\n[doctor] take care\n[patient] that one is a follow-up mm-hmm\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLow back pain.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports he enjoys skiing.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or changes in appetite.\nEyes: Denies changes in vision.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEyes: Pupils equal and reactive to light. Visual acuity is 20/20 in both eyes.\nEars: Normal. No abnormalities.\nNeck: Supple. No thyromegaly.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs. No extra heart sounds.\nGastrointestinal: No tenderness to palpation of the abdomen. No hepatosplenomegaly or palpable masses.\nMusculoskeletal: No tenderness to palpation of the lower back. No pain with straight leg raise. No pain with internal or external rotation of the hips. No pain with flexion or extension of the hips. Strength is 5/5 in the lower extremities. No pain with dorsiflexion or plantar flexion of the feet. No numbness or tingling in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the lumbar spine obtained in office today are unremarkable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is experiencing low back pain after twisting while bending over. He denies any prior injury.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I discussed the nature of his symptoms and our treatment options with the patient. I advised him to limit his activity and use ibuprofen or Tylenol for pain relief.\n- Medical Treatment: I am prescribing Flexeril 10 mg daily to help with muscle spasms. I am also ordering an MRI of the lumbar spine to further evaluate the cause of his pain.\n- Additional Testing: I am ordering an MRI of the lumbar spine.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on metformin twice daily. He is doing well with his diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue his diet and exercise regimen. I also advised him to continue his metformin as prescribed.\n- Medical Treatment: No changes at this time.\n- Additional Testing: I am ordering an A1C to evaluate his current glycemic control.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in one week to review his MRI and A1C results. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLow back pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah is a 65-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for a follow-up visit for his diabetes. He states he has had low back pain for about a week. He denies any trauma or injury. He states he tried some over-the-counter medications but they did not help. He reports that he is having pain in the middle of his back that goes to the left side. He states that if he twists suddenly to the left, it gets worse. He denies any numbness or tingling in his feet. He reports that his diabetes has been stable. He denies any changes in his diet or exercise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nMetformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he has a family history of diabetes. He denies smoking and drinking.\n\nVITALS\n\nPatient is afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Muscle strength 5/5 in all extremities. No muscle atrophy. Reflexes 2+ bilaterally. No sensory 