tivity and use ibuprofen or Tylenol for pain relief.\n- Medical Treatment: I am prescribing Flexeril 10 mg daily to help with muscle spasms. I am also ordering an MRI of the lumbar spine to further evaluate the cause of his pain.\n- Additional Testing: I am ordering an MRI of the lumbar spine.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on metformin twice daily. He is doing well with his diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to continue his diet and exercise regimen. I also advised him to continue his metformin as prescribed.\n- Medical Treatment: No changes at this time.\n- Additional Testing: I am ordering an A1C to evaluate his current glycemic control.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in one week to review his MRI and A1C results. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nLow back pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nNoah is a 65-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for a follow-up visit for his diabetes. He states he has had low back pain for about a week. He denies any trauma or injury. He states he tried some over-the-counter medications but they did not help. He reports that he is having pain in the middle of his back that goes to the left side. He states that if he twists suddenly to the left, it gets worse. He denies any numbness or tingling in his feet. He reports that his diabetes has been stable. He denies any changes in his diet or exercise.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nMetformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he has a family history of diabetes. He denies smoking and drinking.\n\nVITALS\n\nPatient is afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeurological: Muscle strength 5/5 in all extremities. No muscle atrophy. Reflexes 2+ bilaterally. No sensory deficits.\n\nMusculoskeletal: Positive pain to palpation in the lumbar region. No pain to palpation in the thoracic or cervical spine. No tenderness to palpation in the lower extremities.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the lumbar spine ordered.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nMusculoskeletal: Low back strain.\n\nPLAN\n\nI advised the patient to take 400 mg of ibuprofen as needed with Tylenol for pain relief. I prescribed Flexeril 10 mg once daily. I instructed him to avoid any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving after taking the medication. I ordered an x-ray of the lumbar spine. I recommended that he continue with his current diet and exercise. I also recommended that he continue taking his metformin 500 mg twice daily. I advised him to take his blood glucose levels every morning and keep a record of them. I will see him back in 2 weeks for a follow-up.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nPatient was instructed to take 400 mg of ibuprofen as needed with Tylenol for pain relief. He was instructed to avoid any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving after taking the medication. He was instructed to continue taking his metformin 500 mg twice daily. He was instructed to take his blood glucose levels every morning and keep a record of them. He was instructed to continue with his current diet and exercise. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient\'s family owns and runs a bakery.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in feet.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEyes\n- Examination: No redness, discharge, or icterus.\n\nEars, Nose, Mouth, and Throat\n- Examination of Ears: Auditory canal and tympanic membranes are clear.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: No pain to palpation of the upper back. Pain to palpation of the midline of the lower back, especially on the left. No pain to palpation on the right lower back. Mildly positive straight leg rise on the right Positive straight leg rise on the left.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe he has a musculoskeletal low back strain. I do not expect to see anything significant on x-ray, but we will obtain one for further evaluation of any possible disc issues.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed his diagnosis at length today. I explained that physical therapy can help with the pain, but also strengthen some of the muscles in the back. I explained that he needs to be careful while taking medications, such as an overuse of muscle relaxers if he is doing any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving.\n- Medical Treatment: Low back x-ray ordered. Referral to physical therapy provided. He will take 400 mg of ibuprofen up to 3 times per day. Tylenol can be used for severe pain. Prescription for Flexeril also provided.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His diabetes is currently under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle. I encouraged him to try an avoid foods high in sugar. He asked many questions and I feel he is confident in the plan at this point.\n- Medical Treatment: Hemoglobin A1c ordered as he has not had an updated one in almost 5 months. He will continue with his diet and exercise. Continue current dose of metformin for the time being. The patient will be checking his blood sugar in the morning and will log this information for me.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy