oot reveals no erythema or warmth. There is pain to palpation over the right Achilles tendon. The left foot is normal.\nExamination of the right calf reveals no erythema or warmth. There is pain to palpation over the right calf. The left calf is normal.\nThe patient has a positive Simmonds–Thompson test on the right. The left is negative.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the right heel were reviewed and are normal.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nAchilles tendinitis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are consistent with Achilles tendinitis. I have advised him to stop training for a week or two. I have recommended that he take 3 tablets of ibuprofen or Advil every 6 hours. I have also recommended that he attend 2 physical therapy sessions. I have advised him to come back in 7 to 10 days after his first physical therapy appointment. He will follow up with me at that time.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will continue to take ibuprofen or Advil as needed. He will attend 2 physical therapy sessions. He will follow up with me in 7 to 10 days after his first physical therapy appointment.\n\n }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis is a 35-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right heel pain. He reports that the pain is located in the back of his heel and radiates up into his calf. He states that the pain is present after activity and when he first wakes up in the morning. He states that the pain is relieved with activity and then returns after sitting for a long period of time. He denies any trauma to the area.\n\nThe patient reports that he is currently training for a triathlon and has been doing this for about 6 months. He states that he runs about 10 km in the morning, bikes for about 45 minutes to an hour at lunch, and swims