s grossly normal. Musculoskeletal examination of the lower extremities was significant for pain to palpation of the right Achilles tendon.\n\nMedical Reasoning:\nThe patient presents with pain in the back of his heel that radiates up into his calf. The pain is worse after activity and in the morning. The patient reports that the pain is relieved with activity and then returns after sitting for a long period of time. He denies any trauma to the area. The patient reports that he is currently training for a triathlon and has been doing this for about 6 months. He reports that he runs about 10 km in the morning, bikes for about 45 minutes to an hour at lunch, and swims for about 30 minutes to an hour in the evening. He reports that he is doing this 5 days a week and takes one day off. He reports that he does not stretch before or after his workouts. The patient denies any numbness or tingling in his legs. He reports that he is able to run and bike without any issues.\n\nMedical Decision Making:\nGiven the patient\'s history of overuse and lack of stretching, I diagnosed him with Achilles tendinitis. I recommended that he stop training for a week to two weeks. I also recommended that he take ibuprofen or advil three tablets every six hours. I ordered a couple of physical therapy visits. I told him that I would like to see him back in a week to ten days after his first couple of physical therapy appointments. I told him that if his pain persists, we may have to look at more aggressive treatment plans. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis Martin is a pleasant 46-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began 10 days ago while training for a triathlon. He locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his heel, which radiates proximally into his calf. His pain is aggravated by working out and when he first gets up in the morning. If he sits for a long period, his pain will 