ation myocardial infarction. The patient had an RCA stent placed in 2018. The patient reports that she was in her normal state of health until she started having chest pain and was taken to the emergency room. She was diagnosed with another stent placement. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath. She reports that she has been walking her dog daily for a mile and a half to two miles. She denies any leg swelling. She is able to lie flat in bed at night, but uses two pillows. She reports that she has been able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She reports that she has not been watching her salt intake. She reports that pizza is her favorite food. She reports that she has been watching her sugars and has been walking her dog. She denies any shortness of breath.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports that she is compliant with her medications. She takes aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, and Toprol 50 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fatigue.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain or shortness of breath.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports lower extremity swelling.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distension.\n- Carotid: No bruits.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Lower extremities show 1+ pitting edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG reveals normal sinus rhythm with good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes are anticipated given the patient\'s history of recent STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram shows a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. There is moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nThe patient is a 57-year-old female who presents for a follow-up after her recent interior ST elevation myocardial infarction. She has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes.\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a recent interior STEMI and an ejection fraction of 35%. We will continue to monitor her ejection fraction and her mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue to take aspirin 81 mg daily, Brilinta 90 mg twice daily, Lipitor 80 mg daily, and Toprol 50 mg daily.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue cardiac rehab and to exercise regularly. I also encouraged her to work on her diet and to limit her salt intake.\n- Additional Testing: We will repeat an echocardiogram in 2 months.\n2. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension and she is currently tolerating her blood pressure well.\n- Medical Treatment: We will continue her Lisinopril 20 mg daily. She will also start Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to work with a nutritionist to consider other diet options.\n3. Reduced left ventricular dysfunction.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a recent interior STEMI and an ejection fraction of 35%. I believe that her heart function will recover given her compliance with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue to take Lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n4. Moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a recent interior STEMI and an ejection fraction of 35%. I believe that her mitral regurgitation will improve over time.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue to take Lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n5. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been compliant with her medications and her diet.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue to take metformin.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAcute chest pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nJulia is a 65-year-old female who presents to the clinic for a follow-up after an interior ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) where she had another stent placed. She reports that she had been doing well since her last heart attack but recently started having chest pain that would not go away. She reports that it is similar to what she experienced last time. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath since her last visit.\n\nShe states that she has been walking her dog every day and going about a mile and a half to two miles. She is excited for fall and has no plans for the holiday.\n\nShe reports that she has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes. She has noticed some swelling in her legs since being discharged from the hospital. She is able to lie flat at night but has had to sit up a little bit more. She is able to afford her medications and is taking them as prescribed. She has not been watching her salt intake since her last heart attack. She loves pizza and tries are her second favorite food. She is taking metformin and trying to stay active.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nShe is married and enjoys walking her dog.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\n• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or fatigue.\n• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, or palpitations.\n• Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle weakness or joint pain.\n• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.\n• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\n• Neurological: Denies headache, seizure, or stroke.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck Exam: No jugular venous distension (JVD) appreciated. No carotid bruits appreciated.\nRespiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. Systolic ejection murmur 3/6 heard at the left base. No S3 or S4 gallop. No jugular venous distension (JVD) appreciated. No carotid bruits appreciated.\nExtremities: 1+ pitting edema on the right lower extremity. No skin changes or erythema. No clubbing or edema. No radial artery pulses appreciated.\nLower Extremities: 1+ pitting edema on the right lower extremity. No skin changes or erythema. No clubbing or edema. No pedal edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG: Normal sinus rhythm with a good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes consistent with a recent STEMI.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n• Coronary Artery Disease: Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease. Given her recent STEMI and reduced ejection fraction of 35%, she will continue to be monitored. Medical Reasoning: She will continue her aspirin 81 mg daily, brilinta 90 mg twice daily, lipitor 80 mg daily, and toprol 50 mg daily. She will be referred to cardiac rehabilitation for education and exercise.\n\n• Reduced Left Ventricular Function and Moderate Mitral Regurgitation: Medical Reasoning: The patient has reduced left ventricular function and moderate mitral regurgitation. Given her history of coronary artery disease, this is consistent with a stunned myocardium. Medical Reasoning: She will continue her lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n• Acute Heart Failure: Medical Reasoning: The patient has acute heart failure with pitting edema 1+ on her right lower extremity. Medical Reasoning: She will be started on lasix 40 mg daily and aldactone 12.5 mg daily.\n\n• Hypertension: Medical Reasoning: The patient has hypertension. Medical Reasoning: She will be referred to nutrition counseling for dietary modifications. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow up status post inferior STEMI.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nRCA stent 2018\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\naspirin 81 mg daily\nBrilinta 90 mg twice daily\nLipitor 80 mg daily\nToprol 50 mg daily\nlisinopril 20 mg daily\nmetformin\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral leg swelling.\n\nVITALS\n\nWithin normal limits\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: No carotid bruits. A 3/6 systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left base.\n- Examination: Right radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Bilateral lower limb edema. Pitting 1+.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is reviewed and demonstrates a normal sinus rhythm. Good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram is reviewed and demonstrates a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease that we will continue to monitor.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. She will continue Lipitor 80 mg a day and Toprol 50 mg daily. I am going to refer her to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: A recent echocardiogram revealed a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She was able to be treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so I think that the muscle is just stunned. Given her history. it will improve over time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she will recover since she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is retaining fluid.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day. I want to add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. I will order lab work. We will obtain a repeat echo in approximately 2 months.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Blood pressure is well controlled. She will tolerate the Aldactone well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue exercising and to be cognizant of her diet. I explained that there are healthier alternatives available to reduce the amount of preserved meats she consumes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily. The patient received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider what other diet options might be available to her.\n\n5. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue monitoring her blood glucose levels.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin as prescribed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up as instructed.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
