 Patient reports taking fish oil. Patient reports taking Crestor 40 mg once a day.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies weight changes.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain. Denies dyspnea on exertion.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nMusculoskeletal: Denies muscle aches.\nNeurological: Denies headaches.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention. No carotid bruits.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ nonpitting edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nKidney function is stable.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient had a heart attack 6 months ago and underwent stent placement in the left anterior descending artery. He is currently taking metoprolol, Brilinta, fish oil, and Crestor 40 mg once a day. He reports some dizziness with standing. He is still on aspirin.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised him that the stents are permanent and he will need to continue taking aspirin and Brilinta for the next 6 months. I also counseled him on the importance of diet modification and lifestyle changes to prevent future heart problems.\n- Medical Treatment: I will refer him for cardiac rehab. I will also refer him to a dietitian for dietary education. I will continue metoprolol 50 mg once a day, Brilinta, Crestor 40 mg once a day, and aspirin.\n\n2. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has stage 3 chronic kidney disease. He is not taking any nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory medications.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised him to follow up with his nephrologist.\n- Medical Treatment: I will contact Dr. Miller to schedule an appointment for him.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has diabetes. His most recent A1c was 60.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised him that his A1c was high and that he should work on improving his diet.\n- Medical Treatment: I will order another hemoglobin A1c and refer him to a dietitian for dietary education.\n\n4. Depression.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient reports feelings of depression.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised him that medication can help with depression but he should also consider therapy.\n- Medical Treatment: I will refer him to psychiatry for adjustment after a heart attack and mild depression.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nContinue taking metoprolol 50 mg once a day, Brilinta, Crestor 40 mg once a day, and aspirin. Follow up with Dr. Miller for his chronic kidney disease. Follow up with a dietitian for dietary education. Follow up with a psychiatrist for depression. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nPatient reports having a heart attack about 6 months ago and is concerned about the medications he is taking.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports a history of coronary artery disease, for which he had a heart attack 6 months ago. He had a stent placed in his left anterior descending artery. He also reports a history of diabetes. He reports that he had an a1c of 60 and a glucose of 130. He reports that he is not taking any medication for his diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is a retired teacher. He reports that he lives with his dog and is a fan of the San Francisco 49ers.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nExamination of the neck is unremarkable. Examination of the heart reveals a 3/6 systolic ejection murmur at the left base. Examination of the lower extremities reveals 1+ non-pitting edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nPatient had a kidney function test which was stable. He has stage 3 chronic kidney disease. His kidney function was normal.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nPatient reports having a heart attack about 6 months ago and is concerned about the medications he is taking. The patient is on a fair amount of medications. He is taking aspirin, brilinta, and crestor. He is concerned that the crestor may be causing muscle aches. He reports that he gets dizzy when he stands up quickly. He is taking metoprolol 50mg daily. The patient asks how long he will have to take the stent. The doctor explains that the stent is permanent and that he will have to take aspirin for the rest of his life and brilinta for another 6 months. He is concerned about replacing the stent. The doctor explains that he will not have to replace the stent and that he will have to follow up with the cardiologist. The patient is concerned about his diabetes. He reports that he had an a1c of 60 and a glucose of 130. He reports that he is not taking any medication for his diabetes. The patient reports that his a1c was not where he wanted it to be but his glucose is coming in at 1:30. The doctor orders a referral for diabetic education. The patient reports that he had a blood test. He reports that his kidney function was stable. The doctor notes that his kidney function was stable. The patient reports that he has stage 3 chronic kidney disease. He reports that he is not taking any pain medication. The doctor recommends that he take tylenol. The patient reports that he has some puffiness in his legs. The doctor notes that this is due to the medications. The patient reports that he is a fan of the San Francisco 49ers. The doctor asks if he is excited about the super bowl. The patient reports that he is a fan of Jimmy G. The doctor recommends that he talk to his nephrologist. The patient reports that he has not seen his nephrologist in a year. The doctor recommends that he follow up with his nephrologist. The patient reports that he is not doing well since he had his heart attack. The doctor asks if he is having any suicidal thoughts. The patient reports that he is just feeling down. The doctor notes that he is not having any suicidal thoughts. The patient reports that he lives with his dog. The doctor recommends that he talk to a psychiatrist. The patient agrees to talk to a psychiatrist. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nFollow-up.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports having a heart attack 6 months ago, coronary artery disease, diabetes.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of stent placement at the left anterior descending artery.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is from the west coast and is a San Francisco 49ers fan. He lives with his dog.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking aspirin, Brilinta, fish oil, Crestor, metoprolol 50 mg once a day,\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports non-pitting lower extremity edema. Denies muscle aches.\nNeurological: Reports dizziness and headaches.\nPsychiatric: Reports depression. Denies suicidal ideation or homicidal ideation.\n\nVITALS\n\nVital signs look good with blood pressure of 127/80.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention or carotid bruits.\n\nRespiratory\n- Assessment of Respiratory Effort: Normal respiratory effort.\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Slight, 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: 1+ non-pitting lower extremity edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nLab results are reviewed and show stable a creatinine level.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is doing very well and has been compliant with all medications. A slight 3/6 ejection murmur was heard on exam, however, this is stable and has been present in the past. He is experiencing 1+ non-pitting edema, but this seems to be medication related.\n\n- Patient Education and Counseling: Patient was advised that his stents will remain in place permanently and that they typically do not require replacement. We discussed it is common to experience dizziness when standing quickly and that this should resolve once he is adjusted to his medications. We also discussed that cardiac rehab will be beneficial in helping him become more active and confident with exercising. He was advised to watch for symptoms such as chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion as this would need further testing such as a stress test.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to cardiac rehab was provided. A refill for metoprolol 50 mg once a day and Crestor 40 mg once a day was also provided. He is also advised to continue aspirin daily long term as well as Brilinta for the next 6 months.\n\n2. Stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient\'s most recent creatinine levels are stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of following up with nephrology.\n- Medical Treatment: He will follow up with his nephrologist, Dr. Miller.\n\n3. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient reports most recent hemoglobin A1c was 16.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diabetes management and how plays an important role in his coronary artery disease. He was advised that his hemoglobin A1c goal will be 6.\n- Medical Treatment: Repeat hemoglobin A1c was ordered today. A dietitian referral for diabetes education was also provided. We will contact his primary care physician regarding his insulin regimen.\n\n4. Mild depression:\n- Medical Reasoning: Patient reports feeling depressed and having a hard time adjusting since having his heart attack. He mentions he is also lonely and does not have a support system.\n- Medical Treatment: Referral to psychiatry was provided today.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
