ht elbow was reviewed and is normal.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow contusion.\n2. Hypertension.\n3. Diabetes mellitus.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. We discussed that his right elbow pain is consistent with an elbow contusion. I have recommended that he take 800 mg of ibuprofen twice daily to help with pain and inflammation. I have also recommended icing his right elbow 3 times per day for 20 minutes at a time. I have advised him to avoid playing lacrosse with his son for the next couple of weeks to allow his elbow to heal.\nRegarding his hypertension, I have recommended that we continue him on 20 mg of lisinopril daily. He has been compliant with his medication and diet. We will order a hemoglobin A1c to check his blood sugar levels.\nRegarding his diabetes, we will continue him on 500 mg of metformin daily. He denies any recent weight changes, but he admits he fell off his diet during the pandemic. He has been compliant with his diet, and he follows a Mediterranean diet. He denies any recent alcohol or tobacco use.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up in 1 month. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ CHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nPhilip is a 63-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of his right elbow pain. He reports that he hurt his right elbow about a week ago while playing lacrosse with his kids. He denies any recent trauma or falls. He states that his oldest child is 13 years old and the other is 10 years old. He was playing with a 13-year-old. He denies any recent trauma or falls.\n\nHe reports that his elbow hurts at the tip of the elbow and hurts with any movement. He denies any numbness or tingling in his arm. He states that he has tried taking Advil for the pain, but it has not helped much. He reports that the pain radiates down his forearm.\n\n