f at home and has been checking his blood pressure every other day.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient reports a history of hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nThe patient reports taking Bumex 2 mg daily, Cozaar 100 mg daily, and Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies weight changes.\nCardiovascular: Denies chest pain.\nRespiratory: Denies shortness of breath.\nGenitourinary: Denies hematuria.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood pressure is 128/72 mmHg. Heart rate is 79 bpm. Respiratory rate is 26. Oxygen saturation is 99%. No jugular venous distention.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNeck\n- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 2/6 systolic ejection murmur which is stable.\n\nGenitourinary\n- Examination of the Lower Extremities: Trace edema.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEchocardiogram shows a preserved EF of 55%, abnormal diastolic filling, and mild to moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\nMr. Johnson is a pleasant 82-year-old male who presents for follow-up after an acute on chronic heart failure.\n\nAcute on chronic heart failure.\n• Medical Reasoning: This was caused by dietary indiscretion in an uncontrolled hypertension.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Bumex 2 mg daily. Continue to watch diet and avoid salty foods. He should avoid pizza and chicken wings. He should also avoid foods that are high in sodium. He should continue to check his blood pressure at home and contact me if he gains 3 pounds in 2 days.\n• Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of diet and weight control. He was given information about foods that are low in sodium.\n\nHypertension.\n• Medical Reasoning: This is well controlled on current medications.\n• Medical Treatment: Continue Cozaar 100 mg daily. Continue Norvasc 5 mg daily.\n• Medical Reasoning: We will order a renal artery ultrasound to be sure we are not 